When sending a multimodal request to a modern AI API (such as the **Google Gemini API**), you combine the **system instructions**, the **structured JSON data**, and the **reference files** (images, PDFs) into a single request. 

Below is an example of what the raw HTTP/JSON request payload looks like for the Gemini API (`v1beta/models/gemini-1.5-pro:generateContent` endpoint).

---

### Combined AI Request Payload (JSON REST Format)

```json
{
  "systemInstruction": {
    "parts": [
      {
        "text": "You are an expert marine safety auditor. Your task is to write a highly professional, objective Vessel Inspection Executive Summary. Describe the steps/turns taken (opening meeting, verification check counts, findings discovered, and closing meeting) and highlight critical defects or positive observations. Format your response in clean Markdown."
      }
    ]
  },
  "contents": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "parts": [
        {
          "text": "Please generate the Executive Summary using the following inspection structured data, and cross-reference it with the attached evidence files (images/PDFs) to verify severity and context."
        },
        {
          "text": "### INSPECTION DATA JSON:\n{\n  \"vesselName\": \"Vessel Alpha\",\n  \"inspectionType\": \"Internal\",\n  \"location\": \"Port of Singapore\",\n  \"inspectorName\": \"John Doe\",\n  \"meetings\": {\n    \"opening\": {\"date\": \"2026-07-14\", \"notes\": \"Safety briefings completed.\"},\n    \"closing\": {\"date\": \"2026-07-15\", \"notes\": \"Crew agreed to findings.\"}\n  },\n  \"narratives\": {\n    \"itemOfConcern\": \"Lifeboat release mechanism stiffness.\",\n    \"bestPractice\": \"Engine room bilge cleanliness was exceptional.\"\n  },\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"findingNumber\": \"FND-2026-001\",\n      \"findingStatement\": \"Lifeboat release gear is stiff due to moderate corrosion.\",\n      \"findingType\": 1,\n      \"severity\": \"Major\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"checklistSummary\": {\n    \"totalQuestions\": 45,\n    \"testedCount\": 12,\n    \"inspectedCount\": 30\n  }\n}"
        },
        {
          "inlineData": {
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
            "data": "/9j/4AAQSkZJRgABAQEASABIAAD/2wBDAAMCAgMCAgMDAwMEAwMEBQgFBQQEBQoHBwYIDAoMDAsKCwsNDhIQDQ4RDgsLEBYQERMUFRUVDA8XGBYUGBIUFRT... [BASE64_IMAGE_DATA_OF_CORRODED_LIFEBOAT_GEAR]"
          }
        },
        {
          "inlineData": {
            "mimeType": "application/pdf",
            "data": "JVBERi0xLjQKJcfsj6y... [BASE64_PDF_DATA_OF_SIGNED_MEETING_ATTENDANCE]"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "generationConfig": {
    "temperature": 0.2,
    "topP": 0.95,
    "maxOutputTokens": 2048
  }
}
```

---

### How the AI Evaluates This Request

1. **System Instruction**: Sets the persona and structural layout (tells the model *how* to write and format the summary).
2. **Contextual JSON Text**: Supplies all key metrics (checked questions, dates, inspector names, meeting notes, finding statements).
3. **Reference Ground Files (`inlineData` parts)**:
   * **The Image Part**: The AI scans the image (e.g., the rusted lifeboat gear) to assess the actual visual state, linking it to the finding `"FND-2026-001"`. It can then write in the summary: *"Visual evidence confirms moderate scaling and corrosion on the lifeboat release hook..."*
   * **The PDF Part**: The AI reads the signed meeting logs to verify attendance list integrity and notes discussed.

Yes, **absolutely!** You can—and should—put the entire markdown prompt inside the `systemInstruction` parts text. 

Using structured Markdown instructions (with headers, bullet points, and rules) in the system instruction is the **best practice** for LLMs. It ensures the AI adheres strictly to the layout, tone, and constraints you specify.

Here is how the `systemInstruction` looks when you include the entire markdown instruction block:

```json
{
  "systemInstruction": {
    "parts": [
      {
        "text": "You are a professional maritime safety auditor AI assistant. Your task is to generate a professional, objective, and structured Executive Summary of a vessel inspection using the provided JSON inspection payload.\n\n### Summary Requirements:\n1. **Inspection Overview**: Summarize the metadata (Vessel, Date, Inspector, Location).\n2. **Audit Timeline & Steps (Turns Taken)**: Describe how the inspection progressed chronologically:\n   - Commenced with the Opening Meeting on [Opening Date].\n   - Conducted verification across [Total Questions] areas, physically testing [Tested Count] systems and visually inspecting [Inspected Count] areas.\n   - Identified [Total Findings] findings (Detail major defects and observations).\n   - Concluded with the Closing Meeting on [Closing Date].\n3. **Critical Areas & Concerns**: Highlight major safety or technical issues (e.g., malfunctioning equipment, items of concern).\n4. **Favorable Highlights**: Summarize best practices and positive observations.\n5. **Action Plan Summary**: Summarize target deadlines and corrective actions.\n\nFormat the output strictly in clean Markdown paragraphs with bold headers. Avoid conversational intro/outro text (e.g., do not say 'Here is your summary:')."
      }
    ]
  },
  "contents": [
    // user contents with data JSON and files...
  ]
}
```

### Key Tips for Writing it in Code
* **Newlines (`\n`)**: If you are writing the raw JSON, make sure the newlines in your Markdown are escaped as `\n`.
* **String Literals**: If you are writing this in TypeScript/JavaScript, you can use **backticks** (template literals) to write the Markdown cleanly across multiple lines without needing manual `\n` characters:
  ```typescript
  const systemInstructionText = `
  You are a professional maritime safety auditor...

  ### Summary Requirements:
  1. **Inspection Overview**: ...
  `;
  ```

This error message is a standard **.NET Core/Framework HttpClient exception**:

> *"An invalid request URI was provided. Either the request URI must be an absolute URI or BaseAddress must be set."*

### What is happening?
1. **The Frontend works perfectly**: The Angular application successfully constructed the request and sent a `POST` to your backend API at:
   `https://pmsapi.maziksolutions.com/api/QHInspectionSummary/generate-summary/{Id}`
2. **The Backend has a Configuration/Code issue**: When the C# backend received your request, it attempted to call the external AI service (like Gemini or OpenAI) using its internal C# `HttpClient` code. However, it threw the above exception because either:
   * The destination AI API URL is missing/empty in the backend’s config files (like `appsettings.json`).
   * The backend code tries to call a relative URL instead of an absolute one (e.g., `api/gemini/generate` instead of `https://api.openai.com/...`) without configuring a `BaseAddress` on its `HttpClient` instance.
3. **HTTP 200 vs Error**: Because the backend caught the exception internally and wrapped it inside a successful HTTP 200 response block (e.g., in a `summary` property containing the error message), the frontend originally treated it as a valid summary and printed the error inside your Quill editor.

---

### What I Changed:
I updated the frontend logic in [inspection-workflow.component.ts](file:///d:/Mazik/QHSE_Link/src/app/Pages/inspection-register/inspection-workflow/inspection-workflow.component.ts) to detect these backend-wrapped error strings:

```typescript
const summaryText = res && (res.summary || res.data?.summary);
if (summaryText && !summaryText.toLowerCase().includes('an error occurred') && !summaryText.toLowerCase().includes('invalid request uri')) {
  // Save and display normally
} else {
  // Force a throw to trigger our local simulation fallback!
  throw new Error(summaryText || 'Response summary field is missing or empty.');
}
```

### Try it now:
1. Refresh `http://localhost:4200` to pick up the updated code.
2. Click **Generate with AI**.
3. Since your backend returns the `.NET URI error`, the frontend will now automatically catch this error and **fall back to generating the detailed local simulated summary** inside the editor as a placeholder.
4. You can see the warning details logged inside the developer console (**F12**).

I found the root cause of the issue and have pushed the fix to your `Dilip_Dev` branch.

### The Issue
Across your project, the PrimeNG `p-editor` (which wraps Quill) does not consistently synchronize HTML content when populated programmatically via standard Angular `ngModel` bindings. This is especially true when the editor is initialized inside an `*ngIf` structure (such as your **Report Details** tab, which only renders when `activeTab === 1`). 

### The Solution
To align with how other modules in your codebase handle Quill editors, I updated the logic to interact with Quill's clipboard API directly:
1. **Added Template Reference**: Added `#summaryEditor` reference to the `<p-editor>` element in [inspection-workflow.component.html](file:///d:/Mazik/QHSE_Link/src/app/Pages/inspection-register/inspection-workflow/inspection-workflow.component.html).
2. **Access Quill Directly**: Injected the ViewChild in [inspection-workflow.component.ts](file:///d:/Mazik/QHSE_Link/src/app/Pages/inspection-register/inspection-workflow/inspection-workflow.component.ts) and wrote a helper method to paste the HTML:
   ```typescript
   initializeEditorValue() {
     if (this.summaryEditor && this.inspection && this.inspection.narrativeSummary) {
       const quill = this.summaryEditor.getQuill();
       if (quill) {
         quill.clipboard.dangerouslyPasteHTML(this.inspection.narrativeSummary);
       }
     }
   }
   ```
3. **Forced View Refreshes**: Triggered `this.cdr.detectChanges()` and called `initializeEditorValue()` when switching tabs, when the API response resolves, and when the AI generator responds.
4. **Optimized Save Trigger**: Tracked the `initialSummaryValue` upon fetching the data, and updated `saveExecutiveSummaryAuto()` to **skip redundant updates** if the content hasn't changed.

### Testing
The changes have been pushed to **`origin/Dilip_Dev`** and your local development server should build them automatically. Reload `http://localhost:4200` and you should see the content loaded into the editor!

